    "body": "The only reason i would want an XML5 standard is that we will be able to parse web page by using an xml parser and xml tools XPath and other, because for now in HTML5 it is recommend (why ???!?!?) that void element doesn't have a auto-close tag, like &lt;br /&gt; is not considered valid, it should be &lt;br&gt; but doing so break the use of a XML parser, and now we need and HTML parser engine that support all the HTML exception. In the early 2000 year, the XHTML standard was recommended by multiple tutorial to use because of the more strict nature of the XML that encouraged to write better HTML code.\r\n\r\nhttps://crisp.tweakblogs.net/blog/321/html5-why-not-use-xml-syntax.html\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1498932/95907353-63154e80-0d69-11eb-8236-8e9a7d8a6a1b.png)\r\nsrc: https://google.github.io/styleguide/htmlcssguide.html#HTML_Validity\r\nbut after we have\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1498932/95907367-69a3c600-0d69-11eb-830f-5eca42cfaf30.png)\r\nsrc: https://google.github.io/styleguide/htmlcssguide.html#Optional_Tags\r\n\r\nthis is non-sense for me, just put the endtag... is not difficult and it is more coherent and readable",
